# The pinned Android NDK release.
#
# Which of these lines belong to the tool. `tools/sync-ndk.sh --accept` rewrites
# FOUR of the five `key = value` lines at the bottom of this file -- release,
# revision, size and sha256 -- and nothing else: every other line, this header
# included, is printed through unchanged. Two rules for anyone editing them:
#
#   1. Keep the ` = ` spacing. Both the script's reader and its writer match a
#      line by `^<key> = `, so `sha256=...` or `sha256  = ` is invisible to
#      them: --accept silently stops rewriting that key, and the next run
#      refuses with "does not carry all five pinned facts", naming a missing
#      fact rather than the missing space that caused it.
#   2. sha1 is written by no run of this repository, on purpose. It is the
#      publisher's number, transcribed from repository2-3.xml, and --accept
#      used to overwrite it with a sha1sum of the local archive -- this
#      machine's number wearing Google's label, and every later run then
#      congratulated itself on matching "the publisher digest in the pin".
#      --accept leaves the line alone and refuses to declare a match it cannot
#      make; replacing it with the digest from repository2-3.xml is the human's
#      step, after an NDK bump.
#
# One line of evidence per fact, in the order a reader checks them:
#
#   release     the NDK's own name for the release   (source.properties, Pkg.ReleaseName)
#   revision    the NDK's own revision                (source.properties, Pkg.Revision)
#   size        the byte length of that archive       (wc -c, and Google's <size> agrees)
#   sha256      that archive, computed locally        (sha256sum)
#   sha1        that archive, as Google publishes it  (repository2-3.xml, <checksum type="sha1">)
#
# Which archive is deliberately not named here: this file records what the
# release IS, while a URL is a fact about where somebody downloads it, and it
# moves on its own schedule. The URL is in .github/workflows/ci.yml
# (NDK_ZIP_URL) and in devenv.nix (ndkUrl); both name the same
# https://dl.google.com/android/repository/android-ndk-r29-linux.zip that the
# values at the bottom of this file were computed from. `tools/sync-ndk.sh`
# fetches nothing either -- it hashes the archive it is handed, or the one it
# finds beside the tree, and compares that against these five lines.
#
# The two digests have different provenances and the difference is not
# cosmetic. Google publishes SHA-1 for NDK archives and nothing else: the
# <checksum> elements in repository2-3.xml are all type="sha1", the
# unsupported-downloads wiki's column header is literally "SHA1 Checksum", and
# a case-insensitive search for "sha256" across that XML returns zero hits.
# Nobody can look up a SHA-256 in Google's metadata. So:
#
#   sha1  is a publisher value, transcribed by a human from repository2-3.xml,
#         and compared locally. It is the only digest here a reader can check
#         against Google.
#   sha256 is computed locally from the archive in front of the script. It is
#         what a Nix `ndkHash` or an SRI string is built from, so it is the one
#         that has to be here, but it is not Google's number and this file does
#         not claim it is.
#
# The two corroborate each other: `size` matches Google's published <size>
# exactly, and devenv.nix pins `ndkHash = "sha256-Sru83IQvPUh5IG6WldUnCWA+Ut1o08H/8Es7Xnowjs8="`
# for the same URL, which Nix verified on fetch to realise this NDK. That SRI
# decodes to exactly the sha256 below.
#
# `tools/sync-ndk.sh` re-resolves all five, fails if any is missing and fails if
# any moves, and requires the unpacked tree to agree with the archive the
# digests are taken from. Revisit every recorded fact when changing the NDK pin.
#
# One further fact is deliberately NOT here, because it can vanish: the per-ABI
# `min_os_version` in meta/abis.json. It is PRESENT in the pinned r29 -- all five
# ABI objects end with it, carrying 21/21/35/21/21 -- so the floor IS compared
# against the real NDK on every run. The script still probes for it rather than
# assuming it, because the key is not guaranteed to survive: Google's
# googlesource mirror of the NDK's main branch already has ABI objects ending at
# `llvm_triple`, and that mirror moves independently of any release. So when the
# key is absent the script says "the per-ABI floor is NOT cross-checked" and
# carries on -- that is neither agreement nor disagreement, and a script that
# treated an absent key as `null` would report all five floors as changed on an
# NDK that changed nothing else. Abi.minApiLevel in src/ndk.zig is a locally
# held copy of that floor, which is why it survives the key's absence.

release = r29
revision = 29.0.14206865
size = 783549481
sha256 = 4abbbcdc842f3d4879206e9695d52709603e52dd68d3c1fff04b3b5e7a308ecf
sha1 = 87e2bb7e9be5d6a1c6cdf5ec40dd4e0c6d07c30b
